## Matched kernels

Peers share the reviewed algorithm, logical data and evaluation phases. Native representation, allocation and reclamation costs remain observable.

**33 workloads · 66 size cases.**

### Warm-AOT summary

Ratios are **Fai ÷ peer; lower is faster**. Aggregates include only this category's cases that clear the floor gate. Arithmetic and geometric means weight unrounded case ratios equally; p90 is nearest-rank across case ratios, not a latency percentile. These are point estimates for the reviewed workload set.

| Peer | Eligible / matched cases | Geometric mean | Arithmetic mean | P90 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| rust | 66 / 66 | 0.500× | 0.500× | 0.500× |
| ocaml | 66 / 66 | 0.400× | 0.400× | 0.400× |

### Eligibility legend

`F/R` means Fai divided by Rust, and `F/O` means Fai divided by OCaml. Lower ratios mean faster Fai execution. The timing floor is measured using the same harness without the workload.

| Eligibility | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `matched kernel` | Peers share the reviewed algorithm and logical data structures, and all clear the timing-floor check. Their ratios qualify for matched-kernel comparisons. |
| `floor: <languages>` | Each named peer's measured harness floor reaches 10% of its elapsed time. Ratios involving those peers are withheld; another peer comparison may still qualify. |

For example, `floor: rust` suppresses F/R while F/O may remain; `floor: ocaml` suppresses F/O while F/R may remain; `floor: fai` suppresses both. `—` means an excluded ratio, rather than a missing measurement. Only eligible ratios from these main kernels enter the summary above; supplemental components have their own section.
